  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a dip day at both stores, but the narrative is cleaner than the headline suggests. Port Washington dropped 18% and Hicksville 17%, but this was a capacity issue, not execution: Port ran just 5 groomers (below the 5.0/day MTD average) while Hicksville ran 4 (at 2.5/day baseline), so on a per-groomer basis Port actually outperformed trailing 30-day norm at $513/groomer versus $479 baseline. The team stayed disciplined\u2014Port's add-on attach jumped to 62% from 47%, and Hicksville drove retail up 33% on a down-transaction day, signaling good front-of-house work when it mattered.\n\nHere's what matters: August is tracking strong. Port is +11% MTD (or +7% DOW-adjusted, meaning real performance gain beyond calendar favorability), and Hicksville is +21% MTD (+18.9% adjusted). Year-to-date Port is up 45% versus 2025. This isn't noise. You should be proud of the month. The pattern across both stores is healthy\u2014more groomer capacity (101 groomer-days Port MTD vs. 87 last month) is converting to real revenue and appointment growth, not just throughput.\n\nOne thing to watch: Hicksville has zero bather hours in the pipeline. That's structural constraint limiting grooming capacity at that location and depressing core groom revenue ($1,510 yesterday vs. $2,030 last Thursday). If bather coverage is a staffing or scheduling challenge, flag it now. Port Washington's retail and add-on momentum is real; Hicksville's +33% retail suggests the same opportunity exists there if you unlock grooming capacity. Port's lapse pool ($296K annual value) and Hicksville's ($91K) represent material reactivation upside.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Today Port has 7 groomers and 7.0h bathers; Hicksville has 4 groomers and 0.0h bathers; next 7 days Port schedules 38 groomer-days and Hicksville 18, with zero bather support at Hicksville through next Friday."
